{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":302821,"name":"Hood Chatham","username":"hoodmane","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/hoodmane/{size}/4037_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-01T16:29:20.586Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThis is the second discuss thread for \u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0818/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ePEP 818\u003c/a\u003e. The previous thread is \u003ca href=\"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python/105530/22\"\u003ehere\u003c/a\u003e. I got the following feedback:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eVarious name changes were requested, which I implemented.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eThe PEP was too long, so I reduced the length by about half by removing implementation details from the spec section.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI also added support for proxying and conversion of buffers, and for asyncio including an event loop, conversion between Python and JavaScript awaitables, and deferred destruction for arguments to an asynchronous JavaScript function called from Python.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn terms of motivation, I got confirmation that the maintainers of Emscripten-forge (an alternate Emscripten Python runtime) strongly support the proposal. They don’t have an easy way to use Pyodide’s foreign function interface currently so they reimplement it. But they would much prefer to share code, and this proposal is the most direct way to do that.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-10-01T16:29:20.586Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":32,"reads":199,"readers_count":198,"score":244.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":109311,"topic_slug":"pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2","display_username":"Hood Chatham","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://peps.python.org/pep-0818/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 818 – Adding the Core of the Pyodide Foreign Function Interface to Python | peps.python.org","clicks":41},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python/105530/22","internal":true,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 818: Adding the Core of the Pyodide Foreign Function Interface to Python","clicks":13},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python/105530/24","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"PEP 818: Adding the Core of the Pyodide Foreign Function Interface to Python","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":4}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":4043,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2/109311/1","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":4}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":4,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":302828,"name":"David Brochart","username":"davidbrochart","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/davidbrochart/{size}/32621_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-01T17:08:25.752Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eRegarding the asyncio integration, I’m wondering if we could make it more generic to allow integration with other Python event loops (for instance, Trio). It seems the only asyncio-specific code is \u003ccode\u003eensure_future\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eadd_done_callback\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eFuture\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAlso IIUC promise cancellation doesn’t really exist in JavaScript (even with abort signals), so I’m wondering what implications this has on the Python side.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-10-01T17:08:25.752Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":187,"readers_count":186,"score":97.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109311,"topic_slug":"pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2","display_username":"David Brochart","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":4}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":79314,"hidden":false,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2/109311/2","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":4}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":4,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":302830,"name":"Hood Chatham","username":"hoodmane","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/hoodmane/{size}/4037_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-01T18:24:46.826Z","cooked":"\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eif we could make it more generic to allow integration with other Python event loops\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou mean something similar to \u003ccode\u003eset_asyncgen_hooks\u003c/code\u003e? I think it makes sense, I will add it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAlso IIUC promise cancellation doesn’t really exist in JavaScript (even with abort signals), so I’m wondering what implications this has on the Python side.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWell abort signals allow cancellation of certain promises, in the sense that when used the promise will be rejected \u003cem\u003eand\u003c/em\u003e the IO work that the promise is waiting for also stops. But we don’t have any integration with Python cancellation and I don’t really know how to do such integration. For the most part the implication is that when a Python task is cancelled, the current IO that it’s waiting on keeps going. When it finishes, no further work will happen on the cancelled Python task. But the current IO job is not affected.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt would be possible to introduce some API like \u003ccode\u003eCancellablePromise(promise, on_cancel)\u003c/code\u003e. Then you could do\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003econtroller = AbortController.new()\nresp = fetch(some_url, signal=controller.signal)\nwrapped = CancellablePromise(resp, controller.abort)\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnnoyingly this essentially has to be done from user code. But I think it can be implemented downstream.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-10-01T18:24:46.826Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":165,"readers_count":164,"score":37.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":109311,"topic_slug":"pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2","display_username":"Hood Chatham","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":4043,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2/109311/3","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":302833,"name":"David Brochart","username":"davidbrochart","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/davidbrochart/{size}/32621_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-01T21:26:41.188Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eFor integrating any Python event loop, I just meant that the Python code of the \u003ccode\u003eWebLoop\u003c/code\u003e should not depend directly on asyncio, but should use an API that could be implemented using asyncio or e.g. Trio. But given that AnyIO plays this role at the Python level, maybe this is not so important.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe way I see cancellation in JavaScript using abort signals is that the code has to be cooperative all the way down, and in general it is not. So a lot of IO work can be done even though it was cancelled on the Python side. There is an interesting blog post about that \u003ca href=\"https://frontside.com/blog/2023-12-11-await-event-horizon/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ehere\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":4,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-10-01T21:26:41.188Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":144,"readers_count":143,"score":73.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":109311,"topic_slug":"pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2","display_username":"David Brochart","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://frontside.com/blog/2023-12-11-await-event-horizon/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"The await event horizon in JavaScript","clicks":11}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":79314,"hidden":false,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2/109311/4","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303406,"name":"David Brochart","username":"davidbrochart","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/davidbrochart/{size}/32621_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-10T08:16:16.763Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eFWIW there is this \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/arthur-tacca/trio/tree/pyodide-support\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003eTrio branch\u003c/a\u003e which allows running it in Pyodide, and this demo:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003caside class=\"onebox allowlistedgeneric\" data-onebox-src=\"https://arthur-tacca.github.io/trio/\"\u003e\n  \u003cheader class=\"source\"\u003e\n\n      \u003ca href=\"https://arthur-tacca.github.io/trio/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003earthur-tacca.github.io\u003c/a\u003e\n  \u003c/header\u003e\n\n  \u003carticle class=\"onebox-body\"\u003e\n    \n\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://arthur-tacca.github.io/trio/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003eTrio in the browser\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/h3\u003e\n\n\n\n  \u003c/article\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv class=\"onebox-metadata\"\u003e\n    \n    \n  \u003c/div\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv style=\"clear: both\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-10-10T08:16:16.763Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":4,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":5,"reads":104,"readers_count":103,"score":60.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109311,"topic_slug":"pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2","display_username":"David Brochart","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://github.com/arthur-tacca/trio/tree/pyodide-support","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"GitHub - arthur-tacca/trio at pyodide-support · GitHub","clicks":3},{"url":"https://arthur-tacca.github.io/trio/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"Trio in the browser","clicks":3}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":79314,"username":"davidbrochart","name":"David Brochart","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/davidbrochart/{size}/32621_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":79314,"hidden":false,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2/109311/5","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303430,"name":"Arthur Tacca","username":"arthur-tacca","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/arthur-tacca/{size}/18399_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-10T23:38:04.525Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThanks David! I actually made that repo with an LLM to research this topic. I guided it through the Trio internals, which I’m very familiar with, but left it to Pyodide-related decisions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe main conclusion is that clearly it’s possible to make a version of Trio that supports Pyodide. But, without the hooks being discussed, awaiting JS async functions from Python or vice-versa is awkward:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003e# Call JS function from Python \nresult = await trio.pyodide.call(js.jsFn, x)\n# Allow Python function to be called from JS\njs.pyFn = trio.pyodide.callable_from_js(py_fn)\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith hooks these can be simplified to:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003eresult = await js.jsFn(x)\njs.pyFn = py_fn\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe LLM suggests the following signatures for the hooks; I don’t know enough about Pyodide to know how sensible they are. With those in place, Trio wouldn’t need to interact with the \u003ccode\u003eWebLoop\u003c/code\u003e at all (but its presence would be harmless).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003ejstypes.ffi.set_awaitable_hooks(\n    js_to_py=...,  # (jsawaitable, done_callback) -\u0026gt; Python awaitable\n    py_to_js=...,  # (pyawaitable) -\u0026gt; JavaScript promise\n)\njstypes.ffi.get_awaitable_hooks()  # -\u0026gt; the current pair, like sys.get_asyncgen_hooks()\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn the topic of abort signals: these align very well with Trio’s cancel scopes. But there doesn’t need to be any specific support in Pyodide for them; normal library code can provide the necessary helpers for translating to/from cancel scopes. The resulting code would look like this:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003e# Call JS function from Python \nwith trio.pyodide.abort_signal() as signal:\n    result = await js.someAsyncFunction(arg, signal)\n# Python function intended to be called from JS\nasync def fn(x, signal=None):\n    with trio.pyodide.cancel_on_abort(signal):\n        ... # becomes cancelled when signal is aborted\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e","post_number":6,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-10-10T23:38:04.525Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":91,"readers_count":90,"score":22.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109311,"topic_slug":"pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2","display_username":"Arthur Tacca","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":26628,"hidden":false,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2/109311/6","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":303503,"name":"Hood Chatham","username":"hoodmane","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/hoodmane/{size}/4037_2.png","created_at":"2026-10-12T15:26:16.511Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI think we can experiment with adding such hooks in Pyodide, and once we’re confident with them there we can add them to CPython. For now I’d prefer to keep the PEP features a subset of what Pyodide does. That reduces the maintenance hazard if we make mistakes in the initial design.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":8,"post_type":1,"posts_count":7,"updated_at":"2026-10-12T15:26:16.511Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":63,"readers_count":62,"score":10.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":109311,"topic_slug":"pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2","display_username":"Hood Chatham","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":4043,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-818-adding-the-core-of-the-pyodide-foreign-function-interface-to-python-2/109311/8","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null}],"stream":[302821,302828,302830,302833,303406,303430,303503]},"timeline_lookup":[[1,13],[5,5],[6,4],[7,2]],"suggested_topics":[],"tags":[],"tags_descriptions":{},"fancy_title":"PEP 818: Adding the Core of the Pyodide Foreign Function Interface to Python (2)","id":109311,"title":"PEP 818: Adding the Core of the Pyodide Foreign Function Interface to Python (2)","posts_count":7,"created_at":"2026-10-01T16:29:20.512Z","views":743,"reply_count":1,"like_count":11,"l